#![enable(implicit_some)]
// THE ONE CATALOG of what a run (a pipe, hose, tube or cable) is made of (2026-10-04; grown into
// the one catalog 2026-11-02, spline routing rung 0, docs/design/spline-routing.md section 5.3).
// One material id resolves to its look, its behaviour and what it may carry; one size id to that
// size's dimensions, its bend radii, and its electrical, fluid or data specs. The cable registry
// that was data/utilities/conduits.ron (v0.604) merged in here as size rows of their materials,
// keeping every id. Read by crates/humanity-core/src/run_materials.rs (disk first, the copy built
// into the exe if this file is missing or does not parse), which the cable and data checks
// (humanity_core::utilities) and the pipe looks (src/ship/pipe_materials.rs) both read.
//
// The pipe body shows its real material everywhere between the marker bands; what flows inside is
// said by the bands alone (data/piping/marking_schemes.ron). No marking standard colours the wall
// material (findings F28, docs/reference/findings/2026-10-04-pipe-marking-standards.md), and pipes
// that need no warning colour "may be painted to match surroundings ... or remain unpainted"
// (GSFC-STD-8006, F4; MIL-STD-101C says the same, F8).
//
// UNITS. Every length is in METRES (fields ending `_m`): outside diameter, bore, bend radius,
// stick length, roughness. Mass in kg per metre, pressure in kPa, temperature in degrees Celsius;
// the merged cable fields keep their own units in their names (amps, volts, ohms per metre, litres
// per minute, megabits per second, milliseconds). A pipe size's DN is its metric nominal size (ISO
// 6708: DN15 is 1/2 inch); `size` keeps the trade's own name for it. Where the source is imperial,
// a bend radius's `as_read` keeps its words.
//
// SOURCES. Every material, size and bend radius row names where its numbers come from: R1 to R53
// (docs/reference/findings/2026-11-01-pipe-and-cable-routing-rules.md; R45 to R53 and the dated
// notes under earlier findings are the 2026-11-02 refutation pass), D1 to D19
// (2026-11-01-manual-door-releases.md), or Ours("why"). A test fails on a row with none, and on
// one naming a finding that does not exist.
//
// FIELD REFERENCE
//   tags[]      the construction words a material may carry and a rule may name (armour, shield,
//               fibre...): id, meaning. A tag used anywhere and not declared here fails the test.
//   kinds[]     which material each of today's four drawn conduit kinds (src/ship/conduits.rs,
//               ConduitKind::id) is drawn in. WHICH kind carries a given content is decided there,
//               including the potable-water rule recorded in that file. These rows go when rung 1
//               gives every run its own material (no source: they are a mapping, not a fact).
//   materials[]
//     id, name
//     behaviour   Rigid (straight lengths and fittings, never bent in place), Bendable (bent with a
//                 bender to at least its radius, or fitted), Flexible (a smooth curve, hanging or
//                 lying where gravity puts it) (design 6.1). A material's class is fixed; a size
//                 with no bender row turns with fittings.
//     form        Pipe (any fluid line: pipe, tube, hose), Cable (wire, cable, fibre), Raceway (a
//                 conduit or tray that holds cables)
//     carries     what it may carry (Electricity, Water, HotWater, Air, Data, Fuel, Nutrient,
//                 Waste); for a raceway, what the cables pulled through it may. The FIRST is the
//                 content the cable and data checks file its sizes under.
//     conductor   for a cable, what conducts: Copper, Aluminum, Superconductor, Glass; for copper
//                 tube, Copper; absent for a wall that conducts nothing modelled
//     tags        construction words from tags[]
//     look        srgb (0-255, converted to linear light: for a dielectric OUR rendition of its
//                 colour; for a bare metal its MEASURED reflectance, since the shader takes a
//                 metal's F0 straight from it: every channel at least 0.04 linear, the brightest at
//                 least 0.5), metallic (0 dielectric, 1 bare metal), roughness (perceptual, 0..1)
//     sizes[]
//       id        unique across the whole catalog (a connection's `spec` names one)
//       size      the trade's name ("1/2", "14/2", "Cat6")
//       label     what a picker shows
//       dn, od_m, width_m (a flat cable's width; od_m is then its thickness, the way it bends),
//       bore_m, stick_m (the length it is sold in: a straight stick, or a coil), kg_per_m,
//       roughness_m (absolute, for friction), pressure_kpa, temp_max_c
//       bend_radii[]   the material's own minimum by method: Bender (with a bender), Free (by
//                      hand, at rest in place), AgainstCoil, Pulling (a cable under tension):
//                      min_m, as_read, source
//       awg (a larger number is a thinner wire; 4/0 is -3), ampacity_a, voltage_max, ohm_per_m
//       (one conductor), shielded; flow_max_lpm; bandwidth_mbps, range_m, latency_ms,
//       rf_emission (0..1); grade (Home, Commercial, Industrial), cost_per_m (credits a metre)
//       A size is offered to the cable and data checks and the connection pickers only when its
//       check's number is known: ampacity_a for electricity, bandwidth_mbps for data,
//       flow_max_lpm for a fluid. Its absence means no source was read, never zero.
//       note, source
//   links[]     radio links (WiFi and the like): a data medium with no run at all, nothing laid,
//               bent or supported, so not a material. id, label, carries, conductor, shielded,
//               grade, cost_per_m, bandwidth_mbps, rf_emission, range_m, latency_ms, note, source.
//
// Copper tube also carries its maker's type stripe and print line (Type L blue, Type M red, F31);
// that thin marking is not drawn yet.
(
    tags: [
        (id: "unarmoured", meaning: "No metal armour over the cable."),
        (id: "braid_armour", meaning: "A metal wire braid over the cable as armour."),
        (id: "armour", meaning: "Other metal armour (interlocked tape, wire)."),
        (id: "tape_shield", meaning: "A metal tape screen over the insulation."),
        (id: "braid_shield", meaning: "A metal braid screen (not armour)."),
        (id: "single_conductor", meaning: "One conductor."),
        (id: "multiconductor", meaning: "Several conductors in one jacket."),
        (id: "twisted_pair", meaning: "Twisted-pair data cable (Cat5e, Cat6)."),
        (id: "coax", meaning: "Coaxial cable."),
        (id: "fibre", meaning: "Optical fibre."),
        (id: "polyimide", meaning: "Polyimide (Kapton) insulated wire."),
        (id: "flame_test_failed", meaning: "Specialty cable that fails the flame test, so a ship needs fire stops along it (46 CFR 111.60-2)."),
    ],
    kinds: [
        (kind: "rigid_copper", material: "copper_l_drawn"),
        (kind: "flexible_hose", material: "hose_rubber"),
        (kind: "power_cord", material: "power_cord"),
        (kind: "data_cable", material: "data_cable"),
    ],
    materials: [
        (
            id: "copper_l_drawn",
            name: "Copper tube, Type L, drawn (hard)",
            behaviour: Bendable,
            form: Pipe,
            carries: [Water, HotWater, Air, Fuel, Nutrient],
            conductor: Copper,
            look: (
                srgb: (250, 209, 194),
                metallic: 1.0,
                roughness: 0.4,
                note: "Measured copper: a metal's colour here is its reflectance, F0 (0.955, 0.638, 0.538) in linear light, sRGB (250, 209, 194) (Real-Time Rendering, 4th ed., Table 9.2). Until 2026-10-04 this row held #B87333, the web 'copper' swatch ConduitKind::color() carried, which as a metal reflected 3% of blue light, less than any non-metal. Installed tube dulls over months as an oxide film grows: that is a darker, rougher FINISH over the metal (the finish layer the findings describe), not a lower reflectance, and it is not drawn yet. The roughness is a lightly worn tube's.",
            ),
            source: [R(1), R(48), Ours("the look is measured copper's reflectance (Real-Time Rendering, 4th ed., Table 9.2); what it carries is copper tube's usual services in a building, not from a finding")],
            note: "Hard tube bends with the correct hand bender up to 3/4 in (the CDA handbook, R1); its table has no drawn row above that, so 1 in and up turns with fittings (fittings_only_drawn_copper_large).",
            sizes: [
                (
                    id: "copper_l_drawn_1_4", size: "1/4", label: "Copper 1/4\" (hard)", dn: 8,
